Heather Coppersmith wrote:
> 
> Okay, so I keep getting burdened and then killed, mostly because
> I'm a packrat and can't seem to make good use of my stashes.
> 
> When do you make a stash?  Right when you're burdened?  When you
> find "an appropriate place" (whatever that may be)?

	In the first few levels, I only carry stuff that can be used
against me (unIDed potions; scrolls...) OR which is likely to benefit
me in the not-too-distant future (a lamp; un-BUCed armor when I'm
already heading for minetown).

	If I'm likely to become burdened, I drop low-value stuff at
the top of a staircase.  (I drop a wizard's spell books at or before
the entrance to the mines.)  If convenient, I can drag a container 
to the stairs and leave the stuff in that.

	When entering the mines with a low-strength character, I
drop everything non-essential at the top of the stairs.

> Where do you make a stash?  In the nearest box or chest?  Near a
> particular dungeon feature? 

	The two key things to know about your loot are the BUC 
status and the price.  The best place to put a stash is right 
beside an altar on a level with a general store.  The standard
minetowns almost always do the job.

> How far do you drag a chest over to that appropriate place?

	The record is probably about half a dozen levels.  As
short-term solutions, I've sometimes left stuff under boulders.
Sacks are OK.  Ice boxes are superior.

> How do you make a stash?  I know that stacking a boulder on top of
> a box or a chest on top of the E-word is the best...

	I leave a container two squares away from an altar.  I don't
bother with boulders or Elbereth.  I've had at least one container
eaten by a gelatinous cube.

> ...I haven't lost anything I've simply left in a big pile next
> to the stairs, either.

	This is a bad idea if you spend time on the level and the
pile has magical stuff usable by enemies.  It's also bad if it has
high-value items which can be eaten by jelly cubes.

> Do you make different kinds of stashes for different kinds of
> items?  A box of polypile fodder on one level and a box of water
> just begging to become holy on another?

	No.  Is there any benefit to this?  You would certainly
spend longer distributing inventory as it comes in, and doing things
like dipping violet gems in cheap potions.

